  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by leveraging the observation that the maximum correlation occurs at specific lags that relate to the distribution of mass in h(x). Our approach uses a multi-resolution strategy that optimizes the function across different grid resolutions to balance between exploitation and exploration. Key innovations include:\n   - Adaptive grid optimization: We evaluate performance across multiple resolutions\n   - Dynamic lag-selection: Instead of projecting uniformly, we identify problem-relevant lags\n   - Multi-start optimization: We explore different initial phases (9 phases of 5\u00b0 each)\n   - Memory-based continuation: Solutions from earlier optimization steps provide feasible starting points\n   - Budget-aware termination: We strictly adhere to time constraints with periodic checkpoints\n\n   Our method specifically targets the known heavy lags around zero in previous solutions. The algorithm will converge by anchoring positive pulses near zero and shifting negative pulses along the x-axis.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   We represent h(x) as a piecewise constant function with n_points samples. The objective is to minimize:\n     C_5 = max_k \u222b[0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   \n   discretized as:\n     C_5 = max_k [ (2/n_points) * (sum of h(x-k)(1-h(x))) ] \n        but note the full convolution gives values for all k from -(n_points-1) to (n_points-1)\n\n   Constraints:\n     For all x: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n     \u2211(h(x)-0) dx = 1  (equivalent to \u2211(h) = n_points/2)\n\n   We use successive resolution doubling (grid refinement), projecting the solution from coarser to finer grids:\n     n0 = 560 (given)\n     Then consider n = n0 * 2^m for m=0,1,2,... until budget exceeds\n\n   Constraints are enforced via:\n     h(x) = clip(h(x), 0, 1)\n     Adjust normalization by: h(x) = (current_sum * target) / (current_sum) if not normalized\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Parent initialization:\n      Start from 9 phasings of the initial_h_values (0\u00b0 to 40\u00b0 in 5\u00b0 increments)\n\n   b. Resolution sequence:\n      For res in [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 444] (n_points, even numbers)\n        Steps:\n          i. If parent available (n0 or grid downsampled), project and repair to n=444\n          ii. Global exploration using SLSQP with dynamic constraints\n          iii. Local refinement using multi-lag gradients focused on critical lags\n          iv. Update feasible solution\n          v. Save best solution at each resolution\n\n   c. Candidate evaluation:\n        c1 = cross-correlation of h(x) with (1-h(x))\n        c5 = max( [c1_k / n_points for relevant lags] ) * (2/n_points)\n      Note: We evaluate only lags where the correlation might be significant based on grid spacing\n\n   d. Constraints handling:\n        Repair function:\n          h = clip(h, 0, 1)\n          s = sum(h)\n          if abs(s - n/2) > 0.1:\n            ratio = (n/2) / s if s!=0\n            h = h * ratio\n            clip(h,0,1)\n            while abs(sum(h) - n/2) > 1e-5:\n               find index with extreme values and adjust balance\n\n   e. Cross-resolution transfer:\n        Project h from n_old to n_new via:\n           h_new = np.zeros(n_new)\n           for i in range(0, n_new, down_factor):\n               if i//down_factor in range(len(h)):\n                  h_new[i] = (h[i//down_factor] + h.get(i//down_factor + 1, 0.5)) if i+1 < n_new else h[i//down_factor]\n           then repair\n\n   f. Budget management:\n        Break if elapsed(budget_s) or (no improvement in 10 evaluations)\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   a. Resolution sequence: [560, 1120, 2240] due to budget constraints\n   b. Starting points (9 phases):\n        angles = np.linspace(0,20,9)*np.pi/5\n        for each angle, shift initial_h_values\n   c. Optimization parameters:\n        SLSQP: maxiter=1500, ftol=1e-6\n        Multi-lag: sample top 5 lags twice (or once)\n        Repair: dynamically based on grid\n\n   d. Convergence management:\n        Restart every 3 iterations if best score doesn't change\n        Use last step as search base but with noise injection\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   a. Time estimates:\n        SLSQP: O(2000 * n^2) = about 2000 * 560^2 ~ 627,200,000 operations per SLSQP \u2192 10-15s per SLSQP call\n        Multi-lag: O(n * n_lag) = 560*10 = 5600 operations\n        So 3 resolution steps could be 30s + 170s + 340s = about 540s (90% of budget)\n\n   b. Checkpoints:\n        Evaluate every 5 iterations and check if time exceeds 300s\n        Early termination if training starts: if elapsed_time() > budget_s - 15s\n\n   c. Handling repair failures:\n        If repair doesn't maintain constraint, use grid interpolation and local smoothing\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   a. Feasibility checks:\n        Ensure 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n        Strictly: sum(h) == n/2 when n is even\n\n   b. Objective consistency\n        Compute C_5 via: max_k (np.correlate(h,1-h,'full') * (2/n_points))\n        Verify against the provided evaluation method\n\n   c. Return format:\n        (h_array, c5_score, n_points)\n        where h_array being the finest resolution found\n\n   d. Matching evaluation code:\n        Ensure np.ceil(sum(h)) <= n/2 and np.floor(sum(h)) >= n/2 - 5e-4 for repair\n\n   Memory management:\n      Only store solutions for the finest resolution\n      Use rolling storage for previous grid solutions",
